<h1>Windows App Development</h1>

<p>
    I&rsquo;m a Windows app developer with close to a decade of experience building desktop
    applications in C# and XAML. I take on contract work in that niche &mdash; the kind of
    Windows-specific problem that is hard to hire for because the pool of people who have
    actually shipped it is small.
</p>

<div class="card card-background">
    <p class="card-title">Availability</p>
    <p>
        I currently take on a limited amount of contract work alongside my role at
        <a href="https://mu.se/">Muse Group</a>, where I lead the Windows and Linux clients for
        <a href="https://www.musehub.com/">MuseHub</a>. I work with clients worldwide and am
        comfortable with distributed teams across time zones &mdash; I&rsquo;m based in
        New Zealand (UTC+12/+13).
    </p>
    <p>
        If you have a Windows desktop problem that fits the work below, <a href="/contact">get in touch</a>.
    </p>
</div>

<section>
    <h2>What I take on</h2>

    <div class="card card-background">
        <p class="card-title">WinUI 3 and Windows App SDK development</p>
        <p>
            Building new WinUI 3 applications, or adding to existing ones. This includes the parts
            of the Windows App SDK that are poorly documented and tend to eat weeks: window
            management and custom title bars, the unpackaged and self-contained deployment modes,
            app lifecycle and single-instancing, and the WinRT interop needed when the managed API
            surface does not go far enough.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card card-background">
        <p class="card-title">WPF and UWP to WinUI migration</p>
        <p>
            Moving an existing WPF or UWP codebase onto WinUI 3 without a rewrite. In practice this
            is mapping controls that have no direct equivalent, replacing the parts of the
            dispatcher, imaging and resource systems that changed shape, and untangling
            <code>DynamicResource</code> theming into <code>ThemeResource</code>. I can do the
            migration itself, or assess the codebase first and tell you what it would take.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card card-background">
        <p class="card-title">XAML implementation from design</p>
        <p>
            Turning a Figma or design-tool spec into XAML that actually matches it &mdash; correct
            spacing and typography, working Light, Dark and High Contrast themes, sensible
            behaviour at every window size, and keyboard and screen reader support that holds up.
            This is the work I have done most of, and the part most teams underestimate.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card card-background">
        <p class="card-title">Packaging, signing and distribution</p>
        <p>
            Getting the application onto users&rsquo; machines. MSIX packaging, self-contained
            Windows App SDK deployment, code signing, installer work, auto-update, and the
            Microsoft Store submission process. Also the smaller distribution problems that only
            show up in production, like mark-of-the-web on downloaded installers.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card card-background">
        <p class="card-title">Performance and diagnostics</p>
        <p>
            Diagnosing WinUI applications that are slow, leaking, or crashing only on customer
            machines &mdash; XAML layout and virtualisation cost, startup time, memory growth, and
            crash reporting that produces something you can act on.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Background</h2>

    <p>
        I lead the Windows and Linux clients for <a href="https://www.musehub.com/">MuseHub</a> at
        <a href="https://mu.se/">Muse Group</a>, an application shipping to a large install base,
        where I also contribute to the shared C++ core and the TypeScript backend. Before that I
        worked on the Windows client for <a href="https://www.staffpad.net/">StaffPad</a> and built
        industrial line-of-business systems for
        <a href="https://www.inex.co.nz/">Independent Extrusions</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Outside of the XAML stack I work in C++, TypeScript and Rust, and spend spare time on
        low-level graphics and embedded programming &mdash; which tends to help when a Windows
        problem turns out to be a native one.
    </p>

    <p>
        You can see the work on my <a href="/projects">projects page</a>, read the technical
        write-ups on my <a href="/blog">blog</a>, or look through my
        <a href="/cv">curriculum vitae</a>.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Getting in touch</h2>

    <p>
        The most useful first message tells me what the application is, what state it&rsquo;s in
        today, and what you want to be true at the end. I&rsquo;ll tell you honestly whether
        it&rsquo;s work I can help with.
    </p>

    <p><a href="/contact">Contact me</a></p>
</section>
